<!-- ═══════════════════════ 1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY ═══════════════════════ -->
<div class="container">
<details class="accordion" open>
<summary class="accordion-header">
<div class="accordion-num">01</div>
<div class="accordion-text">
<span class="accordion-title">Executive Summary</span>
<span class="accordion-subtitle">Key findings distilled from 300 switcher interviews</span>
</div>
<div class="accordion-icon">+</div>
</summary>
<div class="accordion-body">

<p>This study interviewed 300 Canadian adults in British Columbia and Alberta, all of whom had switched their primary personal checking bank within the past 2 years. The sample was quota-balanced across six current primary banks (CIBC, Simplii Financial, RBC, TD, BMO, Scotiabank). Each participant answered 15 questions covering brand mental availability, previous-bank push forces, the switching decision process, current-bank experience, wallet composition, and forward-looking propensity. Responses were coded against an 83-theme codebook (Cohen's Kappa 0.929) with direction and intensity tags.</p>

<h3>Headline findings</h3>

<div class="insight">
<p><strong>1. The decisive axis in Canadian retail banking is digital-challenger orientation vs. traditional-bank orientation.</strong> Ward clustering on 21 MCA dimensions produced a k=2 segmentation where Simplii Financial customers (20% of the sample) separated almost perfectly from Big 5 customers (80%). Silhouette 0.179, Jaccard stability 0.614 — a real but singular axis.</p>
</div>

<div class="insight">
<p><strong>2. Inside the Big 5 cohort, a secondary split emerges between advisor-anchored wealth/business customers and branch-anchored transactional customers</strong> — but the bootstrap stability is 0.409 (below Hennig's 0.60 threshold for publication), so treat this as directional, not definitive. Small business owners (15% of the study sample) drive most of the advisor-anchored segment.</p>
</div>

<div class="insight">
<p><strong>3. Rejection of considered alternatives is driven more by structural concerns than by specific bad experiences.</strong> When participants considered an alternative bank and chose not to pick it, the most common reasons were "no physical branch access" (for digital challengers), "same fee structure as current bank / lateral move" (for Big 5 alternatives), and "dismissive service interaction" (across the board). Secondhand negative stories were rare.</p>
</div>

<div class="insight">
<p><strong>4. Push forces cluster around three distinct tracks:</strong> (a) advisor/mortgage failures ("my advisor left," "mortgage pre-approval felt impersonal"), (b) accumulating fee-value mismatch ("I looked at what I paid over a year"), and (c) digital experience inadequacy ("the app felt like it was trying to catch up"). These tracks map cleanly onto the three most common switching destinations (traditional-with-advisor, no-fee-digital, modern-big-bank).</p>
</div>

<div class="insight">
<p><strong>5. Wallet fragmentation is deliberate, not accidental.</strong> 70% of participants hold products at institutions other than their primary bank — Wealthsimple and Questrade for investing, EQ Bank for savings rates, mortgage brokers for mortgage rates, kept former-bank credit cards for rewards. Each product fragmentation is a rational single-issue decision.</p>
</div>

<div class="insight">
<p><strong>6. Post-switch satisfaction is high but conditional.</strong> In Q14, "probably staying" dominates "definitely staying" across every bank. Switchers explicitly frame their retention as contingent: contingent on fees not rising, on the advisor staying, on mortgage rates being competitive at renewal. This is not loyalty — it's low-friction stability.</p>
</div>

<h3>Strategic implications by stakeholder</h3>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stakeholder</th><th>Primary implication</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Brand &amp; Marketing</strong></td><td>Concentrate CEP-building investment on "everyday spending" and "digital banking" where Simplii-adjacent brands are out-linking the Big 5 among non-customers. For CIBC specifically, the Aeroplan-Costco positioning is a genuine selection-criterion winner but does not survive against "no monthly fee" when a switcher is fee-primed.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Product &amp; Digital</strong></td><td>The two highest-intensity frustration patterns are (a) fee opacity combined with monthly burden, and (b) app technical reliability. Both cross-reference heavily with Q5 switching stories — these are genuine switching triggers, not background noise.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Retail &amp; Growth</strong></td><td>Small business owners (15% of sample) are disproportionately in the advisor-anchored sub-segment and represent a distinct acquisition playbook. The mass-market transactional sub-segment converts on promotional offers + branch convenience — different tactical approach entirely.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

</div>
</details>
</div>

