<p class="source-note">Source: Screener roster + Q1 demographic confirmation. All participants required to have switched primary personal checking within past 2 years.</p>

<h3>2b. Coding &amp; reliability</h3>

<div class="stat-grid">
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">0.929</div><div class="stat-label">Thematic Kappa<br/>(Almost Perfect)</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">0.970</div><div class="stat-label">Direction Kappa<br/>(+/-/~)</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">0.472</div><div class="stat-label">Intensity Kappa<br/>(collapsed to binary)</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">0</div><div class="stat-label">Codes below<br/>threshold</div></div>
</div>

<p>The coding pipeline used two independent Sonnet 4 coders with an Opus 4 arbiter on disagreements. Every meaning unit received a thematic code plus a direction tag (positive, negative, or neutral). Every participant × code pair also received an intensity score (1 brief, 2 elaborated, 3 narrative). Intensity kappa fell below threshold due to the heavily skewed marginal distribution (most scores at level 2), so per Framework §5.7, intensity analyses in this report collapse to binary (high [2+3] vs. low [1]).</p>

<h3>2c. Known study-design gaps</h3>

<div class="caveat">
<p><strong>Sections where data is thin or absent by design:</strong></p>
<ul style="margin:8px 0 0 20px;font-size:13px;color:#78350F;">
<li><strong>No unprompted CEP discovery.</strong> The original B2C framework's Q3 (open-ended "when does banking come to mind?") was removed from this study. Section 3 uses prompted CEP-institution battery (Q2) only — we measured mental availability for 6 pre-set CEPs, not the full consumer-generated CEP inventory.</li>
<li><strong>No spontaneous brand awareness.</strong> The framework's Q2 absolute recall question was removed. We cannot compute Keller's three-tier awareness classification (mentally available / aware-not-linked / unaware).</li>
<li><strong>No absolute brand rejection.</strong> The framework's Q5 "banks you'd never consider" was removed. Section 4 instead uses <strong>evaluation rejection from Q7</strong> — reasons participants who considered an alternative bank in their switching evaluation chose not to pick it. This is arguably richer behavioral data than absolute rejection would have been, but the interpretation is different.</li>
<li><strong>No origin-pathway distribution.</strong> The framework's "how did you first end up at your previous bank" was removed from Q3. Origin stories are missing; only push forces are coded.</li>
</ul>
</div>

</div>
