<!-- ═══════════════════════ 3. BRAND MENTAL AVAILABILITY ═══════════════════════ -->
<div class="container">
<details class="accordion">
<summary class="accordion-header">
<div class="accordion-num">03</div>
<div class="accordion-text">
<span class="accordion-title">Brand Mental Availability</span>
<span class="accordion-subtitle">Prompted CEP-institution associations across 6 banking situations</span>
</div>
<div class="accordion-icon">+</div>
</summary>
<div class="accordion-body">

<div class="methodology-note">
<strong>Methodology.</strong> Split-sample design. Each participant saw 3 of 6 CEPs: Set A (n=150) got everyday spending, retirement/long-term wealth, small business banking; Set B (n=150) got digital banking, mortgage/home purchase, financial advice. For each CEP presented, the participant named the financial institutions that came to mind (no "why" probing, per study design). Counts below are per-CEP, using the subgroup that was asked about that CEP.
</div>

<h3>3a. Mental availability by CEP (institution mention share)</h3>

{Object.entries(ma?.cep_institution_map || {}).map(([cepId, cep]: any) => {
  const splitData = ma?.cep_buyer_nonbuyer_split?.[cepId]?.banks || {};
  return (
  <div style="margin-bottom:36px;">
    <h4>CEP {cepId}: {cep.cep_label} <span style="color:#6B7280;font-weight:400;font-size:12px;">— n={cep.n_participants_saw_this_cep} participants</span></h4>

    <p style="font-size:13px;color:#4A5568;margin-bottom:8px;"><strong>Overall mention rate</strong> across all institutions:</p>
    <div class="bar-chart">
    {cep.institutions.slice(0, 10).map((inst: any) => (
      <div class="bar-row">
        <div class="bar-label">{inst.institution}</div>
        <div class="bar-track">
          <div class="bar-fill green" style={`width:${barWidth(inst.any_pct, 100)}%`}>
            <span class="bar-val">{inst.any_pct.toFixed(0)}%</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="bar-count">first: {inst.first_pct.toFixed(0)}%</div>
      </div>
    ))}
    </div>

    <p style="font-size:13px;color:#4A5568;margin:18px 0 6px;"><strong>Study banks: buyer vs. non-buyer mention rate</strong> (the strategic diagnostic — non-buyer rate is the growth signal)</p>
    <div class="crosstab">
    <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Bank</th><th>Buyer % (n)</th><th>Non-buyer % (n)</th><th>Lift (pp)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {BANKS.map((bank: string) => {
      const d = splitData[bank];
      if (!d) return null;
      const lift = d.buyer_lift_pp;
      // Hide rows where both buyer and non-buyer rates are 0 (bank doesn't serve this CEP)
      if (d.buyer_any_pct === 0 && d.non_buyer_any_pct === 0) return (
        <tr style="color:#9CA3AF;">
          <td><strong>{bank}</strong></td>
          <td colspan="3" style="text-align:left;font-style:italic;font-size:12px;">Not applicable — bank does not serve this CEP</td>
        </tr>
      );
      return (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{bank}</strong></td>
          <td>{d.buyer_any_pct.toFixed(0)}% <span style="color:#6B7280;font-size:11px;">({d.buyers_mentioned_any}/{d.buyers_saw_cep})</span></td>
          <td>{d.non_buyer_any_pct.toFixed(0)}% <span style="color:#6B7280;font-size:11px;">({d.non_buyers_mentioned_any}/{d.non_buyers_saw_cep})</span></td>
          <td class={Math.abs(lift) >= 25 ? 'above-avg' : ''} style={lift >= 25 ? 'color:#2D7A3F;font-weight:700;' : lift < -5 ? 'color:#DC2626;' : ''}>
            {lift >= 0 ? '+' : ''}{lift.toFixed(0)}pp
          </td>
        </tr>
      );
    })}
    </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  );
})}
<p class="source-note">Source: Q2 (CEP-institution battery). Overall mention rate = % of participants who saw this CEP who mentioned the institution. Buyer = participant whose current primary bank is this institution. Non-buyer = everyone else. <strong>Buyer lift &ge; +25pp highlighted green</strong> indicates a large mental-availability gap — the brand is well-known to its own customers but not yet linked to this CEP by non-customers, which is the growth signal per BBH Ch. 6 ("MPen is most useful among non-buyers").</p>

<h3>3b. Average CEPs linked per bank — buyer vs. non-buyer comparison</h3>

<div class="crosstab">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Bank</th><th>Buyer avg CEPs linked</th><th>Non-buyer avg CEPs linked</th><th>Buyer lift</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{BANKS.map((bank: string) => {
  const d = ma?.avg_ceps_linked_by_bank?.[bank];
  if (!d) return null;
  const lift = (d.buyer_avg_ceps - d.non_buyer_avg_ceps).toFixed(2);
  const isPos = d.buyer_avg_ceps > d.non_buyer_avg_ceps;
  return (
    <tr>
      <td><strong>{bank}</strong></td>
      <td>{d.buyer_avg_ceps.toFixed(2)} <span style="color:#6B7280">(n={d.n_buyers})</span></td>
      <td>{d.non_buyer_avg_ceps.toFixed(2)} <span style="color:#6B7280">(n={d.n_non_buyers})</span></td>
      <td class={isPos ? 'above-avg' : ''}>{isPos ? '+' : ''}{lift}</td>
    </tr>
  );
})}
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="insight">
<p><strong>Buyer lift is a diagnostic for brand-customer fit.</strong> Banks with a large buyer-lift mean current customers think of them across many situations, while non-customers rarely do — this pattern suggests the brand has high customer retention but weak acquisition mental availability. The strategic read is "invest in non-customer CEP-building."</p>
