})}
</div>
<p class="source-note">Source: Q5. Stacked bars: <span style="color:#94A3B8">gray = intensity 1 (brief mention)</span>, <span style="color:#F59E0B">amber = intensity 2 (elaborated)</span>, <span style="color:#DC2626">red = intensity 3 (narrative / specific incident)</span>. Intensity-3 mentions are the most likely to genuinely trigger behavior per Singh (1988).</p>

<div class="insight">
<p><strong>The push-force landscape clusters into three tracks:</strong> (1) Fee-value mismatch and accumulated fee burden, often surfaced by life events (retirement, job change) that force financial review; (2) advisor relationship breakdown — specifically advisor departures or replacements with less experienced staff, heavily concentrated in the 51-60 age band and small-business-owner subgroup; (3) app/digital experience inadequacy — both "clunky" complaints and specific technical failures that left participants uncertain about transactions.</p>
