<h3>Brand &amp; Marketing</h3>
<div class="insight">
<p><strong>Strategic:</strong> The primary segmentation axis is digital-challenger orientation, not traditional competitive differentiation. CIBC's strategic asset within this frame is Simplii — it's already winning the ~20% of the market that wants fee-free digital banking backed by Big 5 infrastructure. Brand-marketing investment should treat CIBC and Simplii as a single portfolio, with CIBC messaging aimed at advisor-anchored wealth/business customers and Simplii messaging aimed at the transactional digital-first cohort.</p>
<p><strong>Tactical:</strong> The macro sentiment data shows three dominant industry critiques — fee/value mismatch, service tiering by wealth, and fees being regressive on lower income. All three are messaging opportunities: CIBC can position Simplii as its "no-two-tier" answer to the service-tiering complaint. The Aeroplan-Costco tie-in surfaces as a selection criterion only for higher-income travel-oriented customers — don't overweight it in mass-market campaigns.</p>
</div>

<h3>Product &amp; Digital</h3>
<div class="insight">
<p><strong>Strategic:</strong> App technical reliability shows up as both a push force (why they left their previous bank) and a current-bank frustration (why they might leave their new bank). This is the single most cross-cutting product signal in the study — the app is the dominant customer interface and its reliability directly drives satisfaction. Treat app technical excellence as table-stakes, not a differentiator.</p>
<p><strong>Tactical:</strong> Q11 frustration patterns suggest the highest-leverage product fixes are (1) fee-management transparency (show customers clearly what they're paying and why, surface waiver eligibility proactively), and (2) business banking tooling for the small-business subgroup (15% of sample but 55%+ of the advisor-anchored segment). The Q13 wallet-elsewhere reasons map directly onto product gap analysis: investment platform, savings rates, and mortgage broker convenience are the three main product leaks.</p>
</div>

<h3>Retail &amp; Growth</h3>
<div class="insight">
<p><strong>Strategic:</strong> Growth comes from acquiring Big 5 customers who are fee-primed and pre-shopping. Section 4's competitive rejection data shows lateral-move concerns ("same fees, different logo") are the #1 reason alternatives get rejected — so acquisition messaging must explicitly differentiate on fee structure, not just tout general "better banking." The one big acquisition path for CIBC proper is the advisor-anchored wealth/business cohort, where CIBC's Wood Gundy and commercial services create a meaningful differentiator against RBC and TD.</p>
<p><strong>Tactical:</strong> Promotional switching incentives appear in Q8 as a selection criterion more often than expected — consistent with the cross-switching-friction literature. Make the switching process itself a product: direct-deposit migration assistance, auto-pay transfer tooling, 12-month fee waiver with clearly-disclosed post-period economics. For small business owners specifically, the #1 acquisition lever is an experienced business banking advisor with authority — <em>not</em> promotional offers.</p>
</div>

<h3>Cross-cutting: retention risk dashboard</h3>
<p>From Q14, participants citing "probably leaving" within a year of switching are the at-risk cohort. Cross-reference with their Q11 frustrations to identify which specific fixes would retain them. The highest-risk combination is: intensity-3 Q11 frustration + &ldquo;probably leaving&rdquo; + holding products elsewhere (Q13) = customers already mentally halfway out the door.</p>

</div>
